Cool work by charlie. The main lesson is that a model's inability to store a new fact isn't just about capacity, but that gradients for route selection are constantly overwriting the part of the training process needed to recover that fact.

Interestingly, the amount of update seems to depend not on how well the fact was initially stored but on the specific future training applied to the next fact. This suggests that forgetting may be path-dependent, with each gradient update potentially affecting future accessibility to memories.

@oneill_c discusses whether new facts can be integrated into an LLM's weights without breaking the model. Their findings indicate that this process is more complex than expected, favoring approaches like compressed kv caches and in-context learning for continual learning over direct weight updates.
